//BUG="an unknown escape is an error in a char literal but literal in a string"
//ERR any
//MSG="Unrecognized escape character"

// Not in FEEDBACK.md. map_escape_char knows \n, \", \\ and \'. In a character
// literal an unknown escape is rejected — see
// 00_lexical/56e_unknown_escape_in_char.cpl — but inside a string the
// backslash is silently kept verbatim, so "a\tb" is the four characters
// a \ t b. Someone reaching for a tab gets a backslash and no diagnostic.
//
// Either reject it in both places (what this case asks for) or support \t in
// both; the one thing worth avoiding is the current split. If you add \t,
// this becomes an //OUT case instead.

fn main
    out "a\tb"
